    "## Conclusion\n",
    "\n",
    "This project explored multiple approaches for solving the Smart MCQ Solver task, progressing from traditional text-similarity methods to semantic embeddings, zero-shot classification, retrieval-augmented generation, supervised transformer fine-tuning, recurrent neural networks, and ensemble learning.\n",
    "\n",
    "The initial baselines provided useful reference points. TF-IDF cosine similarity achieved a MAP@3 of 0.2955, while the Sentence Transformer achieved 0.4155 and zero-shot BART-MNLI achieved 0.5471. The RAG pipeline substantially improved the validation MAP@3 to 0.8850.\n",
    "\n",
    "The supervised models performed strongly on the leakage-free validation split. Fine-tuned DistilBERT achieved a MAP@3 of 0.9206, while the custom BiLSTM achieved 1.0000. The weighted ensemble achieved 0.9986, which was slightly below the BiLSTM and therefore did not improve the best local validation result.\n",
    "\n",
    "The common MCQ-level evaluation also showed the same ordering. BiLSTM achieved 1.0000 accuracy and Macro F1, while DistilBERT achieved 0.8698 accuracy and 0.8665 Macro F1, and RAG achieved 0.8283 accuracy and 0.8275 Macro F1. Error analysis found 0 incorrect Top-1 predictions for BiLSTM, 28 for DistilBERT, and 62 for RAG on the 361-question validation set.\n",
    "\n",
    "However, the local validation results did not translate directly to the unseen Kaggle test set. The final Kaggle submission achieved a public MAP@3 score of **0.75353** and a public rank of **592**. This validation-to-leaderboard gap is an important result of the project because it demonstrates that very high performance on a fixed validation split does not necessarily imply equivalent generalization to the competition test distribution.\n",
    "\n",
    "Weights & Biases was used to track the trainable models and maintain common evaluation runs for BiLSTM, DistilBERT, and RAG. This provided a record of training behaviour and enabled consistent comparison of the models using common MCQ-level metrics.\n",
    "\n",
    "Overall, the project showed that model complexity alone does not guarantee better generalization. The BiLSTM was the strongest model on the local validation split, while the final Kaggle result highlighted the importance of robust validation design, reproducibility, and evaluation on genuinely unseen data."
